[0.066, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m●\u001b[3G\u001b[39mDeclined\u001b[12Gto\u001b[15Ganswer\u001b[22GClaude's\u001b[31Gquestions:\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · Who makes a venue's `production` rows (Ore's furs, Forge's grain), and who owns the output? (Regional employer \u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153m(Recommended) / The broker produces)\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B· No wage amounts or batch lengths are authored, and raw production has no recipe. How should W04 handle payroll? \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[39m   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153m(Daily batches, wag\u001b[26G share / Split: payroll later)\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B· How does a region's household pool buy its essentials (and optional goods, the venue's `demand` rows) when goods \r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m \u001b[3G   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mtrade in whole totes but each interval's need is fractional? (Through the book (Recommended) / Direct, fractional)\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[39m   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153m· The eight-good trading fixture trades no grain, water or medical supplies, so its two regions could never meet a \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bneed. What should happen there? (Need not required / Add essentials / Let them starve)\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;255;255;255m●\u001b[39m Your first three answers are noted:\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B-\u001b[5G\u001b[1mProducer:\u001b[22m a regional employer per region,\u001b[47Gselling\u001b[55Gto\u001b[58Gthe\u001b[62Gbroker\u001b[69Gthrough\u001b[77Gthe\u001b[81Gbook.\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B-\u001b[5G\u001b[1mWages:\u001b[22m daily batches, each with a wage bill that is an authored share of the output's value.\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B-\u001b[5G\u001b[1mHouseholds:\u001b[22m they buy whole totes through the book and\u001b[59Gconsume\u001b[67Gthe\u001b[71Gexact\u001b[77Gamount\u001b[84Gfrom\u001b[89Ga\u001b[91Gpantry.\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1BWhat would you like to clarify about the trading fixture? Its two regions (Ore Highlands and Brine Coast, 25,000\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1Bpeople each) trade only metals, furs, salt, lime, glassware and soap. Without grain, water or medical supplies they\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1Bcan never meet a need.\u001b[K\u001b[27;1H\u001b[24;3H\u001b[?25h"]
[0.036, "o", "\u001b]0;✳ Claude Code\u0007"]
[0.003, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m●\u001b[39m Declined to answer Claude's questions:\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · Wh\u001b[11G \u001b[13Gakes a\u001b[20Gvenu\u001b[25G's `production` rows (Ore's furs, Forge's grain), and who owns the output? (Regional employer \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B(Recommended) / The broker\u001b[33Gproduces)\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m· No wage amounts or\u001b[27Gbatch\u001b[33Glengths are authored, and raw production has no recipe. How should W04 handle payroll? \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B(Daily batches, wage s\u001b[29Gare / Split: payroll later)\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m· How\u001b[12Gdoes a region's household pool buy\u001b[47Gits es\u001b[54Gentials (\u001b[64Gnd\u001b[67Gopti\u001b[72Gnal\u001b[76Ggoods, the venue's `\u001b[97Gemand` rows) when goods \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Btrad\u001b[12Gin whole to\u001b[24Ges but\u001b[31Geach inte\u001b[41Gval's need \u001b[53Gs fr\u001b[58Gctional? (Through the book (Recommend\u001b[96Gd) / Direct, fractional)\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m· The\u001b[12Geight-g\u001b[20God tr\u001b[26Gding\u001b[31Gfixture tra\u001b[43Ges no gra\u001b[53Gn, water or m\u001b[67Gdic\u001b[72G supplies, so its two regions could never meet a \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bneed. What should happen there? (Need not required / Add essentials / Let them starve)\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;255;255;255m●\u001b[3G\u001b[39mYour first thre\u001b[19G answers are noted:\u001b[39G\u001b[K\r\u001b[4C\u001b[1B\u001b[1mProducer:\u001b[22m a regional employer per\u001b[39Gre\u001b[42Gion, selling to the br\u001b[65Gk\u001b[67Gr\u001b[69Gt\u001b[71Grough the b\u001b[83Gok.\u001b[K\r\u001b[4C\u001b[1B\u001b[1mWages:\u001b[22m daily batches, each\u001b[32Gwi\u001b[35Gh a wage bill that is an authored share of the output's\u001b[91Gv\u001b[93Glue.\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B-\u001b[